---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import NestTool from "../tools/NestTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="nest">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Drop a design and get a full sheet of it. Set the material you are putting on the bed, how far apart the
        copies should sit and how much to keep clear of the edge, and the tool says how many fit — with every
        engraving <strong class="text-slate-300">still an engraving</strong> and every cut line still a cut line.
    </Fragment>

    <NestTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </NestTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Rows, and why that is the honest answer</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Copies are packed as bounding boxes in rows across the sheet. Real nesting — sliding one
                        outline into the hollow of another, turning each part to whatever angle wastes least — is
                        NP-hard, and every honest implementation is a solver that runs for minutes and still cannot
                        promise it found the best layout.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        What is here is what a person does by hand: put them in rows. For the parts a laser actually
                        cuts, that lands within a few per cent of a hand layout and it is instant. Where rows are a
                        bad fit — a big L-shaped part, a crescent — it is <em>visibly</em> bad on the canvas rather
                        than quietly bad in the file, and you can see that in a second and go and do it by hand.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The operations survive</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        This is the whole reason to use a tool rather than copy-paste in a laser program. A design's
                        colours say what the laser should do with each part of it — that is how LightBurn, Falcon
                        Design Space and this kit's own exports all group a job — and those colours are read back in
                        and kept.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Twenty copies of a keychain whose lettering has quietly become a cut line is twenty ruined
                        blanks, and nothing on screen tells you until the sheet is on the bed. If a design's colours
                        mean nothing to the tool, it says so rather than guessing: an unrecognised palette comes out
                        on one unnamed operation for you to assign, instead of an engraving cut clean out of the
                        sheet.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The gap is not decoration</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        At zero, two copies' cut lines fall on top of each other: the beam goes down the same slot
                        twice, the edge scorches and the two parts can weld back together at the bottom. Two or three
                        millimetres is enough. It is also where the sheet gives when it warps as it heats.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The margin has a job</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Two things live in it: whatever is holding the sheet down, and the last few millimetres of the
                        bed where the beam is least square to the material. The sheet and the margin are both drawn as
                        dashed guides and neither is in any export — they are where the material is, not something to
                        burn.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">All or none, on the turn</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Every copy is the same shape, so mixing orientations packs no tighter — it only makes the
                        grain run two ways. The design is laid on its side only when the whole sheet fits more that
                        way, which is rarer than it sounds: the counts are symmetric until the truncation bites.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                "Sheet used" is how much of the sheet the copies' bounding <em>boxes</em> cover — not how much material
                is consumed. The space inside a design's own outline counts towards it and is still there when the
                cutting is done. Kerf is not compensated: every copy comes out a beam's width smaller than drawn, the
                same as it would on its own.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
